    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. can you go a bit deeper into the actual signaling cascade after leucine hits the cell? specifically how it moves from the amino acid transporters to the actual activation of the mTORC1 complex at the lysosomal membrane. i want to make sure i can explain the mechanism clearly to the client.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Once leucine crosses the amino acid transporter (CID/SLC38A2), it binds to Sestrin1 at the ribosome, which senses ATP levels and releases the inhibition of the GTPase TSC2. Simultaneously, leucine binding to Sestrin1 and Sestrin2 at the lysosome triggers the lysosomal large GTPase Rheb-GTP to migrate into the cytosol. This active Rheb-GTP then directly binds to and phosphorylates TSC2, completely unlocking the mTORC1 complex to stimulate downstream protein synthesis pathways like S6K1 and 4E-BP1. You can explain this simply as the cell \"checking in\" on leucine abundance to confirm enough energy is available before allowing the heavy machinery of protein construction to start at the lysosome.",
      "source": "target",
