    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it, thanks. one more thing—if the client is struggling to hit their leucine targets through protein alone, would you recommend leucine-enriched supplementation or is it better to just increase total protein intake to avoid any potential issues with nitrogen excretion? also, do you have any insights on how high-intensity training timing might influence this specific pathway's sensitivity?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Supplementation is not strictly necessary if the client can reach the activation threshold (~20–25g) through whole food, but it is an effective strategy to ensure immediate post-workout leucine availability without caloric bloat or potential digestive discomfort from high single-serving protein loads. Timing high-intensity training immediately before a leucine-rich meal can prime the mTOR pathway through exercise-induced inflammation, making the subsequent protein intake more potent than the same nutritional intake alone. While increased nitrogen excretion is a minor concern with low-protein diets, over-supplementation often leads to diminishing returns where excess amino acids are either burned for energy or excreted rather than used for muscle growth.",
      "source": "target",
